<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mattavilasa Prahasana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Mattavilasa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mattavilasa Prahasana, 'The Farce of Drunken Sport', is a one-act <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> comedy written in the early seventh century by the Pallava king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahendravarman-i">Mahendravarman I</a>. It mocks a drunken Kapalika ascetic, a greedy Buddhist monk and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashupata-shaivism">Pashupata</a>, who quarrel over a lost skull begging bowl in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>. It is one of the earliest surviving Sanskrit farces and is still performed in the Kutiyattam tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mattavilasa-prahasana.jpg" alt="Mattavilasa Prahasana" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original uploaded by: Sreekanthv · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mani_damodara_Chakyar-mattavilasa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
